    <h1>🐱 Shikaku Rules — The Complete Guide</h1>
    <p style="text-align:center;color:#6f5651;margin-bottom:1rem;">Four rules, one unique solution. Everything you need to know before you draw your first rectangle.</p>

    <section class="seo-content">

      <p>Shikaku is a rectangle-partition puzzle created by Nikoli, the Japanese publisher behind Sudoku and Akari. The rules fit on an index card, yet they produce puzzles that range from thirty-second warm-ups to dense 8×8 boards that reward careful deduction. This page lays out every rule precisely, walks through concrete 5×5 examples, and clears up the misconceptions that trip up new players. If you would rather learn by doing, our <a href="/how-to-play-shikaku/">beginner's guide</a> takes a hands-on approach, and you can <a href="/">play Shikaku online</a> free at any time.</p>

      <h2>The Grid</h2>
      <p>A Shikaku puzzle is a rectangular grid — GridPaw boards range from 4×4 to 8×8. Some cells contain a <strong>number</strong>; the rest are <strong>empty</strong>. There are no walls, shaded cells, or pre-drawn lines. The numbers are the only information you get, and they are all you need.</p>
      <p>Two things are true of every valid Shikaku grid:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The numbers sum to the cell count.</strong> On a 5×5 board the numbers add up to 25; on a 6×6 board, 36. This is a consequence of the rules, not a separate rule, but it is a useful check.</li>
        <li><strong>Each number is the seed of exactly one rectangle.</strong> The count of rectangles in the finished solution equals the count of numbers on the board. If there are six numbers, you will draw six rectangles — never more, never fewer.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Four Core Rules</h2>

      <h3>Rule 1: Every rectangle contains exactly one number</h3>
      <p>Each rectangle you draw must enclose one — and only one — numbered cell. A rectangle with no number is invalid because nothing defines its size. A rectangle with two numbers is invalid because it can only satisfy one of them. The number may sit anywhere inside the rectangle: corner, edge, or center. Its position does not matter; its presence does.</p>

      <h3>Rule 2: The rectangle's area equals its number</h3>
      <p>The number tells you how many cells the rectangle covers. A cell marked 6 must be enclosed by a rectangle of exactly 6 cells: 1×6, 6×1, 2×3, or 3×2. A cell marked 5 must be a 1×5 or 5×1 strip, because 5 is prime. A cell marked 1 is always a single-cell square. The shape is up to you as long as the area is right and the rectangle fits inside the grid.</p>

      <h3>Rule 3: Rectangles cannot overlap</h3>
      <p>No cell may belong to two rectangles. Once a cell is enclosed, it is spoken for. This is the rule that turns Shikaku into a tiling problem — every rectangle you place removes cells from the pool available to its neighbors, which is exactly what makes deduction possible.</p>

      <h3>Rule 4: Every cell must be covered</h3>
      <p>When you finish, there can be no empty cells left over. The rectangles tile the entire grid edge to edge. If you have placed a rectangle for every number and one cell remains uncovered, at least one rectangle is in the wrong place.</p>

      <h2>Rule Examples on a 5×5 Grid</h2>
      <p>Consider a 5×5 board with columns labeled A–E (left to right) and rows 1–5 (top to bottom). Six numbers are given: <strong>3</strong> at D1, <strong>1</strong> at E1, <strong>9</strong> at A2, <strong>2</strong> at E3, <strong>6</strong> at B4, and <strong>4</strong> at D4. The numbers sum to 25, so the grid check passes. Here is how each rule shapes the solution.</p>

      <h3>Example: the 1 at E1 has one shape</h3>
      <p>By Rule 2, a 1 covers exactly one cell. Draw a 1×1 box around E1. There is nothing to decide — ones are free moves, and you should always take them first.</p>

      <h3>Example: the 9 at A2 has one legal fit</h3>
      <p>Nine cells can be arranged as 1×9, 9×1, or 3×3. The strips are too long for a 5-wide board, so the 9 must be a 3×3 square containing A2. That square could theoretically sit in several positions, but only A1–C3 (columns A–C, rows 1–3) avoids running off the left edge or swallowing another number. Rule 1 and the grid boundary together force it.</p>

      <h3>Example: the 3 at D1 — two shapes, one survivor</h3>
      <p>A 3 is prime, so it is a 1×3 strip. Horizontally it could be B1–D1 or C1–E1; vertically it could be D1–D3. Rule 3 eliminates the horizontal options: B1 and C1 belong to the 9's square, and E1 belongs to the 1. Only the vertical strip D1–D3 remains. Notice that the 3 was solved not by looking at the 3 itself, but by looking at what its neighbors had already claimed.</p>

      <h3>Example: the 6 at B4 — eight shapes to check</h3>
      <p>A 6 can be 2×3 or 3×2 on this board (1×6 does not fit). Counting every position that contains B4 gives eight geometric candidates. Six of them reach up into row 3 or higher, where they would overlap the 9's square (A1–C3) — Rule 3 rules them out. That leaves two 2×3 options along the bottom: A4–C5 or B4–D5. The second one would take D4, which holds the 4 — a Rule 1 violation. So the 6 is A4, B4, C4, A5, B5, C5.</p>

      <h3>Example: the 4 and the 2 fall into place</h3>
      <p>With columns A–C fully claimed in rows 4–5, the 4 at D4 can only be the 2×2 square D4, E4, D5, E5. The 2 at E3 then has exactly two free cells to work with: E2 and E3, a vertical domino. Rule 4 is satisfied — all 25 cells are covered, six rectangles for six numbers, and the puzzle is complete.</p>

      <h3>Quick reference: shapes by number</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Number</th><th>Possible rectangles</th><th>Note</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>1×1</td><td>Always a single cell — free move</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2, 3, 5, 7</td><td>1×n strip only</td><td>Primes have one shape in two orientations</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>1×4, 2×2</td><td>Two shapes; the square is common on small boards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>1×6, 2×3</td><td>Strip rarely fits on grids under 6 wide</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>1×8, 2×4</td><td>On boards under 8 wide, always 2×4</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9</td><td>1×9, 3×3</td><td>On boards under 9 wide, always 3×3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12</td><td>1×12, 2×6, 3×4</td><td>Most flexible common number — check all three</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Common Misconceptions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Myth: You sometimes have to guess.</strong> False. Every properly constructed Shikaku has one solution reachable by pure elimination. If you are guessing, you have missed a forced placement — usually a number with one legal shape or an empty cell only one number can reach.</li>
        <li><strong>Myth: Bigger rectangles are better.</strong> False. There is no scoring for shape, and the area is dictated entirely by the number. A 6 is six cells whether it is a long strip or a compact block; the puzzle decides which, not your preference.</li>
        <li><strong>Myth: The number must be in the corner of its rectangle.</strong> False. The numbered cell can be anywhere inside the rectangle. Assuming it must be a corner will make many puzzles look unsolvable.</li>
        <li><strong>Myth: Rectangles can share a cell along their border.</strong> False. Rectangles are made of whole cells, and each cell belongs to exactly one rectangle. Two rectangles can sit side by side, but they never share a cell.</li>
        <li><strong>Myth: Squares don't count as rectangles.</strong> False. A square is a rectangle with equal sides. 2×2, 3×3, and 1×1 are all legitimate Shikaku shapes.</li>
        <li><strong>Myth: Empty cells at the end mean the puzzle is broken.</strong> Almost always false. Leftover cells mean a rectangle is misplaced. Remove the rectangle nearest the gap and re-examine its alternatives.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why the Rules Guarantee a Unique Solution</h2>
      <p>Puzzle designers verify each Shikaku with a solver before publishing it, and GridPaw does the same automatically — every board is generated from a known tiling and checked for uniqueness. That guarantee is what lets you commit to a rectangle with confidence: if you have eliminated every other shape for a number, the one that remains is not a good guess, it is the answer. Practising this habit is the fastest path from Tutorial boards to Legend difficulty. For technique-by-technique advice, see our <a href="/tips/">Shikaku tips and strategies</a>; for a hint on a specific board, the <a href="/solver/">solver</a> can reveal a single correct rectangle without spoiling the rest.</p>

